## Rose Gold vs White Gold vs Yellow Gold vs Platinum

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Metal</th><th>Colour Grade Min</th><th>Upfront Cost</th><th>Lifetime Cost</th><th>Aesthetic</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>14k Rose Gold</strong></td><td>G (nuanced)</td><td>Baseline</td><td>Polish only ($150–$300)</td><td>Romantic warm pink</td><td>Our rose pick. Nuanced colour rules.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>14k Yellow Gold</strong></td><td>H–J</td><td>Baseline</td><td>Polish only ($150–$300)</td><td>Classic warm</td><td>Most forgiving colour.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>14k White Gold</strong></td><td>F–G</td><td>Baseline</td><td>$500–$1,500 re-plating</td><td>Classic cool</td><td>Rhodium maintenance required.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Platinum</strong></td><td>F–G</td><td>+$300–$600</td><td>Polish only ($150–$300)</td><td>Lifetime cool white</td><td>No maintenance beyond polish.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** Rose gold has the second-lowest lifetime cost (behind yellow gold and tied with platinum maintenance) because no rhodium plating is needed. The upfront cost is baseline. The colour-matching rules are more nuanced than yellow or white — 5 Clash Patterns to avoid. For buyers who want warm metal + nuanced romantic aesthetic + no plating maintenance, rose gold is the answer.

